{"lib": "field", "chunk": {"id": "fed-phmsa-excavator-196-2", "state": "US", "tier": "core", "title": "Federal enforcement for pipeline damage by excavators: 49 CFR 196 Subpart C", "topic": "Digging near pipelines: federal excavator rule", "text": "Federal enforcement against excavators who damage pipelines is in 49 CFR Part 196, Subpart C. PHMSA uses its normal process (49 CFR Part 190): a notice of probable violation, 30 days to answer or ask for a hearing, a final order, and a chance to ask for reconsideration (196.203). It may assess a civil penalty (196.205); the maximum amounts are the ones Congress set in 49 U.S.C. 60122 (196.207). It can also ask the Attorney General to sue in federal court for an injunction, penalties and punitive damages (196.209), and criminal penalties are possible under 49 U.S.C. 60123 (196.211). If an operator fails to answer a locate request or marks its pipeline wrong, PHMSA can enforce the operator's own damage prevention duties (196.111, pointing to 49 CFR 192.614 and 195.442). For a crew this means: a pipeline hit with no ticket, digging before the marks, or not reporting the hit can be a federal case as well as a state one. Dollar amounts were not read here; they change each year with inflation.", "source": "49 CFR 196.111, 196.201 to 196.211 (eCFR, up to date as of Oct 22, 2026)", "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-196", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "eCFR current as of October 22, 2026", "confidence": "medium", "kind": "summary", "license": "gov-us"}},
